(a) The wrecker service advisory board shall provide a wrecker service with a hearing prior to a suspension, by the marshal's office, of more than seven days duration or revocation of its zone privileges, giving the wrecker service at least three days' notice of the time and place for hearing.
(b) A revocation shall terminate all authority and permission with respect to zone privileges. Any wrecker service whose zone privileges have been revoked shall not be eligible to again apply for such privileges for a period of not less than one year from the final date of revocation.
(c) Any wrecker service whose zone privileges have been revoked or suspended by the marshal's office and/or the wrecker service advisory board may file a notice of appeal with the board of commissioners within ten days from the date of such revocation or suspension by submission of a written request for appeal to the marshal's office. Should the board of commissioners choose to hear the appeal the wrecker service shall be given at least three days' notice in advance of the date of a public hearing with respect to such appeal. The board of commissioners reserves the right not to hear an appeal. If an appeal is heard by the board of commissioners, they may affirm, reverse, vacate, or modify the action appealed.
(d) Due cause for the suspension or revocation of zone privileges shall consist of any of the following, with respect to the wrecker service:
(1) Violation of any laws, ordinances or resolutions regulating such business, or violation of regulations made pursuant to authority granted for the purpose of regulation such business;
(2) The wrecker service has supplied false information in connection with its application or provision of wrecker services;
(3) The licensee has failed to obtain, or has suffered expiration, suspension or revocation of, any paper or document necessary in pursuit of its business as may be required by any officer, agency, authority or office of the county, state or the United States, under authority of any law, ordinance or resolution of the county, state or United States, including without limitation, the requisite business license and insurance required by the article;
(4) The wrecker service has failed to comply with any provision of this article;
(5) The wrecker service has engaged in any fraudulent business transaction which is made punishable by the laws of this state;
(6) The wrecker service has made any misrepresentation of fact, whether through advertisement or through any form of direct communication, oral or written, which is intended to mislead the public or to mislead any party with whom the wrecker service deals in pursuit of its business. The term "misrepresentation of fact" as used herein shall embrace not only express misrepresentations, but also misrepresentations arising by virtue of the wrecker services conduct, including acts and omissions;
(7) The wrecker service fails to maintain adequate equipment or personnel to perform the services required;
(8) The wrecker service fails to properly maintain and provide an impound lot in the assigned zone meeting requirements of this article;
(9) The wrecker service repeatedly fails to respond to calls within a reasonable period of time or lack of response thereof;
(10) The wrecker service has engaged in monitor (scanner) running;
(11) The wrecker service has failed to provide 24-hour, seven days a week service;
(12) The wrecker service has violated rates listed on the approved fee schedule;
(13) The wrecker service fails to properly clear roadway of debris as required by this Code and state and federal laws that may apply;
(14) The wrecker service has engaged in any act or practice designated as unlawful in O.C.G.A. § 10-1-393, the Fair Business Practices Act of 1975; or
(15) The marshal's office may suspend or revoke a wrecker service permit in its discretion for additional due cause not specified herein.
